        "math:radical-equations",
        "cnm_079"
      ],
      "explanation": "The radical \\(\\sqrt{x-1}\\) requires \\(x\\ge1\\). This also ensures that \\(5x-1\\ge4\\), so throughout the real domain,\n\\[\n\\sqrt{5x-1}\\ge2,\\qquad \\sqrt{x-1}\\ge0.\n\\]\nAt \\(x=1\\), the original equation becomes\n\\[\n\\sqrt4+\\sqrt0=2,\n\\]\nso \\(1\\) is a solution. If \\(x>1\\), then \\(5x-1>4\\), making the first radical greater than \\(2\\); adding the nonnegative second radical cannot give \\(2\\).\n\nTherefore the unique solution is \\(\\boxed{x=1}\\), choice D.",
